# Clean Architecture

## Purpose
Backend layering follows Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin): presentation, application,
domain and infrastructure, with dependencies pointing inward and a domain that imports no
framework, ORM or external SDK. This rule holds only what that name leaves open.

## When To Apply
Apply to backend code when creating new modules, services, or features; when deciding where to place a new class or function; when wiring up dependencies between layers. Frontend layering (service → store → component, business logic in the store) is `code-style-patterns.mdc`, not this rule.

## Mandatory Behavior

- **Business rules live in domain entities and domain services**, not in use cases. A use
  case coordinates repositories and domain services and owns the transaction; a controller
  parses input, calls one use case and formats the output.
- **A controller that queries the database is a layer violation**: there is no
  thin-controller exception, and it is rated by Review severities below.
- **A repository is the I/O boundary that gets an interface** (`solid-principles.mdc`,
  D): the interface is declared in the domain, the implementation lives in infrastructure.
- **Each boundary maps its own types.** A persistence row never reaches a controller, and a
  request DTO never enters a domain entity. Request DTOs belong to presentation, use-case
  input and output to application, persistence models to infrastructure.
- **A use case or transaction that changes more than 2 domain aggregates is split.**
- **A module's public API is its index exports.** Importing another module's internal files
  (`b/internal/...`) crosses its boundary.
- **Project size is not an exemption**: the layers apply to a small service too.
- **Layout:** follow the project's existing layout. With none, use
  `src/{domain,application,infrastructure,presentation}/<module>/`.

The check for a change: replacing the database or the transport changes no domain file.

---

## Review severities (shared with reviewers)

`review-architecture` rates layer and dependency-direction findings by this table; `review-backend` uses the same rating for the same condition. Change a row here, never in a reviewer. A layer violation is a maintenance cost, so it is `minor`; `major` needs a named defect (a trigger and a wrong outcome), never size or a project preference.

| Signal | A finding when | Default | Not a finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| Controller queries the database or holds business rules | always | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect | parsing input, calling one use case, formatting output |
| Business rule added to a use case instead of the domain | the change adds the rule to a use case | `minor` | coordination and transaction ownership |
| Domain imports infrastructure, framework, ORM or SDK | always | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect | — |
| Persistence row reaches a controller, or a request DTO enters a domain entity | always | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect | — |
| Repository without a domain interface, or an interface with no I/O or substitution boundary | the repository is an I/O boundary with no interface, or the interface has one internal implementation | `minor` | a plain helper or value object |
| Use case or transaction changes more than 2 domain aggregates | always | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect (for example lost consistency) | — |
| Another module's internal files imported instead of its index exports | the path bypasses the index | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect | — |
| Core or shared module imports feature code | always | `minor`; `major` only with a named defect | — |
| Circular import between modules | always | `minor`; `blocker` when an import resolves to `undefined` on a path the change reaches | — |
